$DIR/main.p:16:24: o was moved into consume (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at line 18, but the deferred function (line 17) uses it when the function returns
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. Passing an owner by value moves it instead of copying it, so
    that what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 18, o holds nothing. The
    function f, which holds a function literal that uses o, is deferred at line
    17 and runs when the function returns, after line 18, so it would use what o
    no longer holds.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Give o a new value after line 18, so that the deferred call finds one:
        o = Buf{}
    Or do the work of the deferred call at the end of the function, before line
    18, instead of deferring it
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:23:24: o may have been moved into consume (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at line 27, but the deferred function (line 25) uses it when the function returns
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. o is moved into consume (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at
    line 27 on some paths to this point and not on others. On those paths o
    holds nothing afterwards, and the compiler cannot let a value be used where
    it may already belong to someone else. The function g, which holds a
    function literal that uses o, is deferred at line 25 and runs when the
    function returns, after line 27, so it would use what o no longer holds.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Give o a new value after line 27, so that the deferred call finds one:
        o = Buf{}
    Or do the work of the deferred call at the end of the function, before line
    27, instead of deferring it
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3302" for a longer explanation)
